These long specs are killing me.

I know that it's the way the AI-assisted programing world is going. We
have the belief that if we can just write enough detail, then we can
one-shot the thing.

It doesn't work for complex systems. See some of Kent Beck's recent
talks.

We need to find a better way of working. I suspect it's something along
the lines of user stories, ADRs, and technical specs.

- user stories provide the behavior
- ADRs provide a record of tech decisions we made along the way
- technical specs define high level constraints

Thoroughly research ways of describing work with LLMs, that's effective
for LLMs to consume, but optimizes for human understanding. Prefer
experience reports and guidance that is focused on iterative development
instead of one-shotting. Provide examples.

Also I want to think about scope... because my sense is that
one-shotting is great for something like unix utilities, which are small
and focused and can be done. For complex systems that are never done - I
doubt that works.
